WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.456
The Data Pipelines module lets you read data from any source of your

00:00:05.456 --> 00:00:10.816
site, transform it, and deliver it as files or content items, without writing code.

00:00:10.816 --> 00:00:15.454
This video shows why it exists, how to design, preview, publish and run

00:00:15.454 --> 00:00:19.910
a pipeline, where its results go, and how to automate and extend it.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:29.521
The data of your site lives in content items, users and saved queries.

00:00:29.521 --> 00:00:33.147
Sooner or later, someone needs it somewhere else:

00:00:33.147 --> 00:00:38.290
a spreadsheet for the team, a feed for a partner, or a file on a server.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.667
Each of those exports usually means custom code, a scheduled job, or a

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.650
script that only one developer understands.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.307
A data pipeline describes the export once, in the admin:

00:00:51.307 --> 00:00:55.528
where the data comes from, how it changes, and where it goes.

00:00:55.528 --> 00:01:00.380
Anyone allowed can preview it, run it, and see what each run delivered.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.429
The Data Sources module provides the data.

00:01:07.429 --> 00:01:11.464
A data source offers data sets, such as the items of a content

00:01:11.464 --> 00:01:15.559
type, the users, or the results of a saved query, and each data

00:01:15.559 --> 00:01:18.348
set describes its fields and their types.

00:01:18.348 --> 00:01:22.680
Other modules, such as reporting tools, read the same data sources.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:28.461
A pipeline connects steps through ports that carry rows or files.

00:01:28.461 --> 00:01:34.412
Sources read rows, transforms change them, file steps write them as CSV, Excel

00:01:34.412 --> 00:01:38.380
or JSON files, and destinations deliver the files.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:43.242
A run streams the rows through the steps in batches, so a large

00:01:43.242 --> 00:01:45.626
export never has to fit in memory.

00:01:45.626 --> 00:01:50.870
It runs in the background, with the access of the user who published the pipeline.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.262
Features add the steps you need:

00:01:54.262 --> 00:01:58.434
the media library, email, content items and workflows.

00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:05.040
The FTP and SFTP uploads are modules of their own, as they rely on third-party libraries.

00:02:09.219 --> 00:02:12.791
The data pipeline features are in the Data category.

00:02:12.791 --> 00:02:15.439
Search for them on the Features page.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.132
Data Pipelines is the core feature.

00:02:19.132 --> 00:02:21.267
The others add steps:

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:26.880
the media library, email, content items, workflows, FTP and SFTP.

00:02:31.082 --> 00:02:36.950
Under Tools, Data Pipelines, the Pipelines page lists every pipeline, with its published

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:41.382
version, its unpublished changes, and the result of its last run.

00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:44.713
Search finds a pipeline by its name.

00:02:45.163 --> 00:02:47.208
Edit opens the designer.

00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:52.863
The Actions menu runs the pipeline now, opens its run history, or deletes it.

00:02:53.314 --> 00:02:54.905
Let's create one.

00:02:54.905 --> 00:02:59.194
Add Pipeline asks for its name, then opens the designer.

00:03:03.394 --> 00:03:07.136
The designer shows the steps on the left, the canvas in the middle,

00:03:07.136 --> 00:03:10.827
and the settings, issues and history of the pipeline on the right.

00:03:10.827 --> 00:03:12.774
A pipeline starts with a source.

00:03:13.224 --> 00:03:16.417
The step opens in the panel below the canvas.

00:03:16.417 --> 00:03:18.420
Pick the data set to read:

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.674
here, the items of the Blog Post content type.

00:03:22.125 --> 00:03:26.303
Pick the fields to read, in their order, and add filters, such as

00:03:26.303 --> 00:03:28.775
the posts published since September.

00:03:30.937 --> 00:03:35.757
The Data tab previews the first rows, with the settings of the draft.

00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:38.327
A preview never delivers anything.

00:03:42.505 --> 00:03:45.685
Click the output of a step to add the next one.

00:03:45.685 --> 00:03:50.065
The menu offers only the steps that accept what the output carries.

00:03:50.517 --> 00:03:57.161
Add calculated fields computes fields from formulas, with text, number, date and logical functions.

00:03:57.161 --> 00:04:02.857
The headline is the title in capitals, and the month is when the post was published.

00:04:03.307 --> 00:04:07.854
The Fields tab shows the fields a step receives and produces, with their

00:04:07.854 --> 00:04:11.177
types, which the designer infers from the formulas.

00:04:11.628 --> 00:04:20.848
Other transforms filter, select, sort, group and summarize, join, combine, deduplicate and limit rows.

00:04:25.034 --> 00:04:30.724
Create a file writes the rows as a CSV, Excel, JSON or JSON Lines file.

00:04:30.724 --> 00:04:33.385
Its options follow the format:

00:04:33.385 --> 00:04:36.194
a workbook has a worksheet name.

00:04:36.645 --> 00:04:40.689
The file name can use placeholders, such as the date of the run,

00:04:40.689 --> 00:04:44.445
the name of the pipeline, or a parameter a workflow passes.

00:04:44.895 --> 00:04:48.530
Save to the media library stores the file in a folder.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:53.195
Its editor warns about the file types the media library doesn't accept.

00:04:55.874 --> 00:04:58.452
A file can go to several destinations.

00:04:58.452 --> 00:05:03.024
Drag Share a download link from the library, and connect the file to it.

00:05:04.605 --> 00:05:09.155
A download link works only for its recipients, once they sign in, and

00:05:09.155 --> 00:05:11.945
stops working after the days you choose.

00:05:12.396 --> 00:05:17.656
The Data tab of a destination shows the file it would receive, without delivering it.

00:05:21.834 --> 00:05:25.601
The draft saves as you work, and runs never use it.

00:05:25.601 --> 00:05:30.094
Publish creates a version, and runs use the published version.

00:05:30.545 --> 00:05:35.465
Versions lists the published versions, and restores any of them into the draft.

00:05:36.146 --> 00:05:38.695
Run queues a run in the background.

00:05:38.695 --> 00:05:44.476
The History tab follows it, and the canvas shows the rows and files each step produced.

00:05:44.926 --> 00:05:49.896
Each run keeps its status, its steps, what it delivered, and a log.

00:05:54.088 --> 00:06:00.045
Run History, under Tools, Data Pipelines, lists the runs of every pipeline.

00:06:00.045 --> 00:06:03.428
Search by pipeline, or filter by status.

00:06:03.879 --> 00:06:06.474
A failed run shows why it failed:

00:06:06.474 --> 00:06:09.999
here, the API of a partner couldn't be reached.

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:16.202
Shared Files lists the files shared through download links, with their recipients, their

00:06:16.202 --> 00:06:18.221
expiry and their downloads.

00:06:18.221 --> 00:06:20.669
A link can be revoked at any time.

00:06:21.121 --> 00:06:24.971
Recipients find the files shared with them on the Files shared with you

00:06:24.971 --> 00:06:27.821
page of the site, and download them once signed in.

00:06:32.021 --> 00:06:36.851
Sources read a data set of any data source, or the rows of

00:06:36.851 --> 00:06:40.851
a CSV, Excel or JSON file of the media library.

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:48.496
Transforms filter rows into matched and unmatched outputs, add calculated fields, select and

00:06:48.496 --> 00:06:55.251
rename fields, sort, group and summarize, join two inputs, combine, remove duplicates, and

00:06:55.251 --> 00:06:57.081
keep the first rows.

00:06:57.831 --> 00:07:04.005
File steps write CSV, Excel, JSON and JSON Lines files, and zip several

00:07:04.005 --> 00:07:06.331
files into one archive.

00:07:07.081 --> 00:07:13.895
Destinations deliver to the media library, download links, email, web APIs, FTP and

00:07:13.895 --> 00:07:20.021
SFTP servers, and content items, which creates or updates items from rows.

00:07:24.221 --> 00:07:28.802
In a workflow, the Run Data Pipeline Task runs a published pipeline.

00:07:28.802 --> 00:07:34.251
It can wait for the run to end, and continue with the Succeeded or Failed outcome.

00:07:35.001 --> 00:07:39.880
It passes parameters, written with Liquid, and steps read them with the Parameter

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.301
placeholder, such as the date a data source filters on.

00:07:44.051 --> 00:07:48.473
The Data Pipeline Run Completed Event starts a workflow when a run ends,

00:07:48.473 --> 00:07:51.251
such as to notify someone when a run fails.

00:07:55.451 --> 00:08:00.905
The DataPipelines section of appsettings.json limits the rows a step holds in memory,

00:08:00.905 --> 00:08:04.261
how long runs are kept, and the rows of a preview.

00:08:05.011 --> 00:08:09.056
The media library accepts Excel workbooks by default.

00:08:09.056 --> 00:08:13.650
To save CSV, JSON or zip files to it, add their extensions to

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.981
its allowed file extensions.

00:08:16.731 --> 00:08:21.980
The All Data Pipelines deployment step exports pipelines without their secrets.

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:27.601
The Data Pipelines recipe step imports them as drafts, to publish on the target site.

00:08:31.801 --> 00:08:35.152
A step type derives from DataPipelineStepType.

00:08:35.152 --> 00:08:41.661
It names its category, describes the fields it produces, and processes the rows batch by batch.

00:08:42.411 --> 00:08:47.817
Register it with AddDataPipelineStep, with the display driver of its editor, and it

00:08:47.817 --> 00:08:50.551
appears in the library of the designer.

00:08:51.301 --> 00:08:54.140
A data source implements IDataSource.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:09:00.611
Once registered, every pipeline, and every other module that reads data sources, can use it.

00:09:02.611 --> 00:09:08.756
Data pipelines turn exports, feeds and transfers into pipelines that anyone allowed can

00:09:08.756 --> 00:09:11.333
design, preview, publish and run.

00:09:11.333 --> 00:09:13.051
Thanks for watching.

